1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,751
And the live demo: both services starting from scratch, a real transfer,

2
00:00:05,751 --> 00:00:09,952
the history it produces, five calls that get rejected,

3
00:00:09,952 --> 00:00:13,687
and proof that the rejected calls changed nothing.

4
00:00:13,987 --> 00:00:15,943
Two processes, two ports.

5
00:00:15,943 --> 00:00:18,878
The account service owns the balances,

6
00:00:18,878 --> 00:00:22,302
the transaction service owns transfers and history.

7
00:00:22,302 --> 00:00:24,258
Both are now listening.

8
00:00:24,558 --> 00:00:28,277
Alice has a thousand, bob two fifty fifty.

9
00:00:28,277 --> 00:00:33,855
These are plain GetBalance calls straight to the account service on 50051,

10
00:00:33,855 --> 00:00:38,503
and you can see them arrive in the account log.

11
00:00:38,803 --> 00:00:42,340
One call to the transaction service on 50052.

12
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:48,531
Behind it: read both balances, debit alice, credit bob, then write two history rows.

13
00:00:48,531 --> 00:00:52,952
Four RPCs on the account service for one client call.

14
00:00:52,952 --> 00:00:58,258
The response carries the transaction id, the status and both new balances,

15
00:00:58,258 --> 00:01:00,911
so no follow-up read is needed.

16
00:01:01,211 --> 00:01:06,925
Alice is down to eight hundred, bob up to four fifty fifty.

17
00:01:06,925 --> 00:01:13,115
The total is unchanged: a transfer moves value, it does not create it.

18
00:01:13,415 --> 00:01:15,771
One transaction id, two histories.

19
00:01:15,771 --> 00:01:19,069
Alice's row says out, bob's says in,

20
00:01:19,069 --> 00:01:22,367
and balance_after matches what GetBalance just reported.

21
00:01:22,367 --> 00:01:26,136
That is the consistency check I care about.

22
00:01:26,436 --> 00:01:28,295
FAILED_PRECONDITION for insufficient funds,

23
00:01:28,295 --> 00:01:33,408
with the actual balance against the requested amount in the detail.

24
00:01:33,408 --> 00:01:37,127
NOT_FOUND for an account that does not exist.

25
00:01:37,127 --> 00:01:40,381
Different codes, because they are different failures.

26
00:01:40,681 --> 00:01:45,794
INVALID_ARGUMENT for a self transfer, caught before any balance is touched.

27
00:01:45,794 --> 00:01:52,301
And a negative amount is rejected too: a negative transfer is a disguised withdrawal,

28
00:01:52,301 --> 00:01:54,626
and withdrawals belong on UpdateBalance.

29
00:01:54,926 --> 00:02:00,135
Carol has zero, and the store refuses to go below zero.

30
00:02:00,135 --> 00:02:07,238
Then a plain read confirms carol is still at zero: the failed call changed nothing.

31
00:02:07,538 --> 00:02:13,139
Final state: one recorded transfer, all the rejected calls absent from history,

32
00:02:13,139 --> 00:02:16,873
and the transaction log showing the same id.

33
00:02:16,873 --> 00:02:21,074
That is the whole failure story in one screen.

34
00:02:21,374 --> 00:02:26,689
What I would defend: status codes instead of strings in a body,

35
00:02:26,689 --> 00:02:30,232
one writer per balance guarded by a lock,

36
00:02:30,232 --> 00:02:33,774
a compensating rollback when the credit leg fails,

37
00:02:33,774 --> 00:02:36,874
and a deadline on every outbound call.

38
00:02:36,874 --> 00:02:40,417
What is honestly missing: persistence, TLS, idempotency keys,

39
00:02:40,417 --> 00:02:43,074
and integer money instead of doubles.

